{
  "dom_id": "step-q84911",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "The axis of symmetry of $y=\\frac{1}{3}(k-x)(5x+3k)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{10}.$ The value of $k=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{10},$ we will set our result equal to $\\frac{1}{10}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=\\frac{1}{3}(k-x)(5x+3k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=k$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3k}{5}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-\\frac{3k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5k}{5}-\\frac{3k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{5} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
